---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="About — Work in Ozaukee">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="lede">
      I work remotely in Port Washington. This is the list I'd built in my head anyway,
      written down.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it's a log and not a directory</h2>
    <p>
      Directories rot. Every listing is a promise to keep it accurate forever, and
      nobody keeps that promise — which is why most local guides are quietly wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      So this is dated entries instead. What a place was like on a given Tuesday morning
      stays true forever. Nothing here needs revising, and nothing here silently decays.
      The summaries you see on each place are assembled from those visits, not typed up
      once and abandoned.
    </p>

    <h2>About the hours</h2>
    <p>
      Hours are the one thing that genuinely does go stale, so every listing shows where
      the information came from and when it was last confirmed. A time confirmed with the
      owner last week and a time copied off a listing site in April are not the same
      claim, and they shouldn't look the same.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here is scraped, and nothing is copied from a listing site without saying
      so. If a time looks old, that's because it is — and you can see exactly how old.
      Honest staleness beats false confidence.
    </p>

    <h2>Why not just use Facebook</h2>
    <p>
      Because plenty of people aren't on it, and increasingly that's a deliberate choice
      rather than an oversight. Right now a lot of small businesses post their hours,
      closures, and changes to a platform you have to have an account to read — which
      quietly locks out anyone who left.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site is the other channel. No account, no app, no algorithm deciding whether
      you see that a place is closed on Monday. Just a page, and
      <a href="/rss.xml">an RSS feed</a> if you want it to come to you.
    </p>

    <h2>If you run one of these places</h2>
    <p>
      Tell me when your hours change and I'll keep the listing current — email
      <a href="mailto:hello@workinozaukee.org">hello@workinozaukee.org</a>. It's free,
      it'll always be free, and it reaches the people who don't see your Facebook posts.
    </p>

    <h2>Working together</h2>
    <p>
      The other half of this: working alone is fine, and working near other people is
      better. If you're remote in the area and want company, get in touch — the plan is a
      standing morning somewhere downtown, same place, same time, no agenda.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
